<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Devlin Report</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1959</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Devlin Report was the report of a British commission of inquiry, chaired by the judge Patrick Devlin, into the state of emergency declared in Nyasaland in March 1959, when African nationalist leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> were arrested. Published in July 1959, it found that Nyasaland had become, no doubt temporarily, a police state, and that most Africans opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>, damaging the federation's credibility.</p></div>
